py Veterans Day  you  good afternoon everyone I'm Tamil  Clayton the excuse me president of the  Yale Veterans Association this is a  great great day and a great event I've  seen these events since 2001 and every  year they get better and more inspiring  and today I have the distinct honor and  pleasure to take a moment to recognize  and thank the person who started this  tradition as well as many other  initiatives at Yale that support our  veteran and military community Linda  lorimer initiated in that great we owe  her so much  initiated and sponsored the veterans day  ceremony when she was university  secretary it is one of the many  initiatives that she sponsored to  recognize Yale veterans and yells  history of support for the defense of  our nation she was instrumental in  restoring ROTC to campus and then  supporting its development so that Yale  is now the gold standard for rotc and  the Ivy League and it's so great to see  the cadets in the midshipman here today  with us without her help you guys and  ladies would not be here as officer  responsible for the association of Yale  alumni she supported and encouraged the  formation of the Yale veterans  association and his subsequent  development as the daughter of a career  naval officer Linda understands the  importance and value of military service  and importantly the need for Yale as a  globally important institution to  support and honor it and she has one of  the most satisfying aspects of my  position in the Y VA is receiving  numerous calls for my counterparts at  Harvard and Princeton asking about yells  success in veteran and military affairs  what's going on in New Haven they ask me  well my response is that the success is  due to the strong support of the  administration and we know that Linda is  a major force in that support Linda on  behalf of the veteran military community  at Yale including the Yale veterans  Network the Yale student veterans  council and the Yale Veterans  Association thank you so much for all  you have done for us we would not be  where we are today without you as we say  in the Navy we wish you fair winds and a  following sea as you leave Yale to go to  your next adventure could you come up to  the stage and Fred Nagle come up to the  stage please there is please accept this  small token of our appreciation for all  the big things that you have done for us  and now I would like Fred Nagle the  chairman of the Veterans Association to  present Linda with our gift when that  you've been the most wonderful godmother  imaginable the daughter of a Rear  Admiral who served in three wars was a  strike force commander you simply made  it all happen you clearly have made a  very perceptible change in Yale  University and you just have to walk  around and see all the uniforms and the  fact that service members are now thank  for their service beyond that the L  veterans has done a lot in and outside  the Yale community but you really made  it happen and I think is a further  tribute to what you've done we now have  developed a very close relationship with  West Point there are several graduate  students who are West pointers here  including the president or vice  president of the Yale Veterans  Association and we're going to have our  second Yale veterans summit at West  Point next April and finally maybe  imitation is the greatest form of  flattery I've been asked to start  another shared interest group based on  the success of the Y VA for the  intelligence community but you were the  godmother of it all  okay my dear friends thank you for your  very very kind words but as Tom and both  of you know I am just a representative  of the many who work to have Yale be  much more conspicuously grateful for the  service of its veterans veterans who are  Yale students yell staff yell faculty  and of course our extraordinary alumni  though others of us who have been the  beneficiary of Veterans Service has has  been said already need really to be  saying thank you every day for putting  your lives on the line to defend our  liberties but at least we have this  occasion once a year to offer what is in  our hearts of really praised as well as  Thanksgiving I know that we today those  of us here but elsewhere recognize that  the alums here are just the tip of the  iceberg for those who render service to  our nation from Yale reminded of it in  one way every day going through memorial  chapel I who had a chance for 18 years  to work most directly with our alumni  recognize how pervasive is among our  alumni body as Tom said my dad was a  career naval officer he started his  military career at the end of World War  two serving on a minesweeper he then was  on the involved as a naval aviator  during the Vietnam War and commanded the  largest aircraft carrier in the world at  the time during very tense times in the  Mediterranean in the 70s he would come  home every night and this is not an  exaggeration he loved flying which was  most of his career but he said what  means most is I'm here to sustain the  peace in just a couple of minutes we  will hear what is always one of the high  point of my years the anthems that Tom  Duffy had a range of the all of our  military services and it will be our  chance as each of the representatives  here from each branch lasseur  stanzas recognized to offer our  heartfelt thanks for their service but  for all they represent for other Yale  women and men here and elsewhere but let  me close with a very special salute to  those students in our ROTC units I am so  proud of you and I am so grateful for  your commitment to our country and for  your decision to come to Yale when I was  a student here there were no ROTC units  when I returned to join the  administration in 1978 there were no  ROTC units I said to myself back then  sort of most of your parents were not  even in college then that I hope that I  would stay at Yale long enough to see  rod see welcome back to this campus we  had to wait a long time but it has been  worth it you cadets and Midshipmen are  the shining reasons for why ROTC is so  important to Yale not simply to our  nation I stand a little taller when I  see you walking down Wall Street going  from one class to the other you remind  us of what service means the commitment  you have made at this early age and we  can't wait to wait a couple of years  when you're out of Pensacola or when you  graduated and you're on your own  deployment to see how you are making a  difference in this new way of leadership  that Yale is regaining again as a navy  junior I hope you understand why I say  Anchors Aweigh to you new future  perspectives to veterans that will be  there serving our nation but I conclude  today and saying mostly thank you to the  veterans of today my heartfelt  appreciation for all you do  you  you  you  Oh  let us pray Almighty God as we remember  those who have served their country with  commitment and with courage we give  thanks for their dedication and  sacrifice we pray for your continued  blessing upon our nation and upon all  the nations of our world as we go from  this place of honored memory we beseech  you to bless us that we may strive with  new dedication for peace in our world  and for justice in our common life all  men please remain standing for the  retiring of colors  thank you everyone this concludes our  veterans day ceremony for this year many  thanks to the planning committee into  our ceremony participants and  particularly thank all of you for coming  out to celebrate our veterans today have  a good day
 

